<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="Research notes by Dr. Lydia Moreno on interpretable machine learning, single-cell genomics, and graph neural networks for 
connectomics.">
  <title>Blog | Lydia Moreno, Ph.D.</title>
  <link rel="icon" href="assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="container nav-wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="index.html" aria-label="Lydia Moreno — home">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">LM</span>
        <span class="brand-text">
          <strong>Lydia Moreno</strong>
          <small>Computational Neuroscience</small>
        </span>
      </a>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href="index.html">About Me</a>
        <a href="blog.html" aria-current="page">Blog</a>
        <a href="publications.html">Publications</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="container page-hero" aria-labelledby="blog-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Notes from the lab</p>
      <h1 id="blog-title">Writing about models, minds, and data.</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Short reflections on interpretability, computational biology, and the practical
        work of turning complex neural data into useful scientific insight.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container section-tight" aria-label="Latest blog posts">
      <div class="blog-layout">
        <div class="post-list">
          <article class="post-card" id="interpretable-deep-learning">
            <div class="post-meta">
              <time datetime="2025-03">March 2025</time>
              <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>Explainable AI</span>
            </div>
            <h2>Building Interpretable Deep Learning Models for Neuroscience</h2>
            <p>
              A model that predicts neural activity is only the beginning. In this note,
              I reflect on treating interpretability as a design constraint: choosing
              representations that can be inspected, testing feature attributions against
              controlled perturbations, and reporting where a model’s explanations become
              fragile.
            </p>
            <p class="post-note">
              Related thread: explainable AI methods for neural decoding and the importance
              of model behavior that neuroscientists can interrogate.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="post-card" id="ml-single-cell-genomics">
            <div class="post-meta">
              <time datetime="2025-01">January 2025</time>
              <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>Single-cell omics</span>
            </div>
            <h2>Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics</h2>
            <p>
              Multi-modal single-cell data offer a remarkable view of cellular identity,
              but integrate them carefully: normalization choices, batch structure, and
              missing modalities all shape the biological conclusions. I outline a
              practical workflow for keeping preprocessing decisions visible and for
              validating clusters against independent evidence.
            </p>
            <p class="post-note">
              Related thread: scalable pipelines for single-cell genomics and spatial
              transcriptomics.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="post-card" id="scaling-gnn-connectomics">
            <div class="post-meta">
              <time datetime="2024-11">November 2024</time>
              <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>Connectomics</span>
            </div>
            <h2>Scaling Graph Neural Networks for Connectomics</h2>
            <p>
              Connectomic graphs are large, sparse, and biologically structured. Scaling
              graph learning here is not only a hardware problem; it is a question of
              preserving local circuit motifs while sampling efficiently across the
              reconstruction. I discuss representation choices that helped our team infer
              synapse types and connectivity patterns at useful scale.
            </p>
            <p class="post-note">
              Related thread: graph neural networks for circuit analysis and the NeuroGraph
              project.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <aside class="blog-sidebar" aria-label="Blog sidebar">
          <section class="sidebar-card" aria-labelledby="topics-title">
            <h2 id="topics-title">Topics I write about</h2>
            <ul class="tag-list">
              <li>Explainable AI</li>
              <li>Neural decoding</li>
              <li>Biomedical imaging</li>
              <li>Single-cell omics</li>
              <li>Connectomics</li>
              <li>Open science</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section class="sidebar-card" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
            <h2 id="reading-title">Explore the research</h2>
            <p>
              Peer-reviewed work from the lab spans computational neuroscience,
              neuroinformatics, and single-cell biology.
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="publications.html">View publications</a>
          </section>

          <section class="sidebar-card" aria-labelledby="discuss-title">
            <h2 id="discuss-title">Join the conversation</h2>
            <p>
              Have a question about a post or an idea for a collaboration?
            </p>
            <a class="text-link" href="mailto:lydia.moreno@riverbend.edu">Get in touch</a>
          </section>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="container footer-inner">
      <p>© 2025 Lydia Moreno. Research profile and selected work.</p>
      <div class="footer-links">
        <a href="index.html">About Me</a>
        <a href="blog.html">Blog</a>
        <a href="publications.html">Publications</a>
        <a href="mailto:lydia.moreno@riverbend.edu">Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
